```java
public interface RateLimiter {
    boolean tryAcquire();
    boolean tryAcquire(int permits);
}
```

Returning a boolean rather than blocking or throwing keeps the caller in control of what happens on rejection — retry, queue, or reject the request outright — a decision the limiter itself shouldn't make.

```java
public class TokenBucketRateLimiter implements RateLimiter {
    private final long capacity;
    private final double refillPerMs;
    private double tokens;
    private long lastRefillMs;

    public synchronized boolean tryAcquire(int permits) {
        refill();
        if (tokens >= permits) { tokens -= permits; return true; }
        return false;
    }

    private void refill() {
        long now = System.currentTimeMillis();
        tokens = Math.min(capacity, tokens + (now - lastRefillMs) * refillPerMs);
        lastRefillMs = now;
    }
}
```

Capacity bounds the burst size; the refill rate sets the steady-state rate. The two are independent knobs — a large capacity with a slow refill allows big occasional bursts but a low sustained rate.

```java
private final Lock lock = new ReentrantLock();

public boolean tryAcquire(int permits) {
    lock.lock();
    try {
        refill();
        if (tokens >= permits) { tokens -= permits; return true; }
        return false;
    } finally {
        lock.unlock();
    }
}
```

Refill-then-check-then-deduct is a single compound operation that must run atomically — two threads each refilling and then checking tokens independently could both see enough tokens and both succeed, over-granting past the actual capacity. The lock, or an equivalent CAS retry loop, closes that race.
